export const CANONICAL_QUEUE_JSON = "[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A1\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 1,\n    \"title\": \"Operating leverage\",\n    \"figure\": 14,\n    \"pinned\": true,\n    \"note\": \"pinned post; attach figure 14\",\n    \"body\": \"Restaurants don't die in a bad year. They die in a slow February.\\n\\nYour rent, insurance, salaried managers, and equipment notes don't\\ncare how many covers you did. Those costs are fixed.\\n\\nThat means every cover above break-even is almost pure profit,\\nand every cover below it is almost pure loss.\\n\\nThere's a name for this. It's called operating leverage, and it's\\nthe same structure that makes airlines and software companies\\nbehave the way they do.\\n\\nNobody tells kitchen people this. It explains almost everything.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 5,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-08-31\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A16\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 16,\n    \"title\": \"Overhead allocation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your catering arm might be losing money and your P&L would\\nnever show it.\\n\\nOverhead — rent, utilities, salaried managers — has to get\\nassigned somewhere. Most operations spread it by revenue share\\nbecause that's easy.\\n\\nBut if catering uses half the walk-in and a third of the prep\\nlabor while making a fifth of the revenue, allocating by revenue\\nmakes it look profitable and makes the dining room look worse\\nthan it is.\\n\\nHow you split the shared costs decides which part of the business\\nyou think is working.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 230,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D3\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Sealed pieces\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Just finished a business degree and the strangest part is how\\nit arrives.\\n\\nMacro, then five weeks later organizational behavior, then\\naccounting. Each course sealed off from the others.\\n\\nThe connections are real. Comparative advantage in economics\\nis the same idea as a make-or-buy decision in strategy. But\\nthe person teaching macro isn't the person teaching strategy,\\nso nobody says it.\\n\\nBy the capstone it's all in twenty separate boxes and you're\\nasked to use it at once.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 35,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A45\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 45,\n    \"title\": \"Core competency\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A core competency has to pass three tests, and \\\"we make good\\nfood\\\" passes none of them.\\n\\nIt has to open access to more than one market. It has to matter\\nvisibly to the customer. And it has to be hard for a competitor\\nto copy.\\n\\nGood food fails the third. Anyone can hire your cook.\\n\\nA hiring and training system that reliably turns inexperienced\\npeople into competent line cooks in three weeks passes all three.\\nThat one is worth building. The recipe isn't.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 692,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C30\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 30,\n    \"title\": \"The pilot that proves nothing\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I designed a pilot, looked at it honestly, and concluded it would\\nhave proven nothing.\\n\\nSmall group, no comparison, measured by asking people whether\\nthey felt more confident, run in a place where I'm present every\\nday.\\n\\nEvery one of those produces a positive result almost regardless\\nof whether the product works.\\n\\nThe uncomfortable part is that the flawed version would have\\nproduced a better-sounding case study than the rigorous one will.\\n\\nWhich is a good description of why most vendor case studies read\\nthe way they do.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 522,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B43\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 43,\n    \"title\": \"Work rules\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The standard for whether your handbook rules are lawful under\\nfederal labor law has flipped twice in eight years and may flip\\nagain.\\n\\nThe current framing asks whether a rule would tend to chill a\\nreasonable employee from exercising Section 7 rights — and the\\nemployer bears the burden of justifying it.\\n\\nThat reaches ordinary policies: confidentiality, media contact,\\ncivility, recording bans.\\n\\nThe board's composition drives this standard, and it changes with\\nadministrations.\\n\\nPractical read: write rules narrowly and say why they exist. A\\nrule with a stated business justification survives more versions\\nof this test than a broad one does.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 737,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-08\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C23\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 23,\n    \"title\": \"Content is the product\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I thought I was building software. I'm mostly building content,\\nand the software is how it gets delivered.\\n\\nAn empty training platform is worth nothing. What an operator is\\nactually buying is the sequenced, correct, industry-specific\\nmaterial that would take them 200 hours to write.\\n\\nWhich reframes everything. The moat isn't the app — that's the\\npart that's copyable. The moat is that I can write a station\\nmodule correctly because I've worked the station.\\n\\nI've been optimizing the wrong half.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 396,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-08\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B27\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 27,\n    \"title\": \"Essential functions\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Write your job descriptions before you need them, because after\\nyou need them nobody believes them.\\n\\nAn ADA analysis asks whether the employee can perform the\\nessential functions of the position, with or without reasonable\\naccommodation. Essential means fundamental, not marginal.\\n\\nCourts look at what the job actually requires — including a\\nwritten description prepared before the position was advertised.\\n\\nA description written the week of a dispute carries roughly the\\nweight you'd expect.\\n\\nTwo hours, once, per position. It's the cheapest documentation in\\nthe building.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 455,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A2\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Contribution margin\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The $28 steak is not your best-selling item. The $3 soda is.\\n\\nSteak: $28 price, maybe $9 food cost. $19 contribution.\\nSoda: $3 price, maybe $0.30 cost. $2.70 contribution.\\n\\nNow count how many of each you sell in a night.\\n\\nContribution margin per item x volume is the number that\\nmatters. Menu price alone tells you almost nothing.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 22,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A31\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 31,\n    \"title\": \"Overjustification\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Paying for something people already did for free can make them\\nstop doing it.\\n\\nYou had a crew that stayed late to help each other close. You\\nintroduced a $15 bonus for it. Within a month, nobody stays late\\nwithout asking about the bonus.\\n\\nExtrinsic reward can crowd out the internal reason. The behavior\\ngets re-filed from \\\"who we are\\\" to \\\"what it pays.\\\"\\n\\nThe bonus was cheap. What it replaced was not.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 465,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-10\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D5\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Recurring concepts\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Ten ideas showed up in three or more of my courses wearing\\ndifferent vocabulary each time.\\n\\nOpportunity cost. Comparative advantage. The agency problem.\\nFixed vs variable cost behavior. Correlation isn't causation.\\nStructure follows strategy.\\n\\nLearning those six properly once is worth more than learning\\ntwenty things partially.\\n\\nEverything else in a business degree is application.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 69,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-10\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A60\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 60,\n    \"title\": \"Crashing vs fast-tracking\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Two ways to speed something up and they fail differently.\\n\\nCrashing: add resources. More people on prep. Costs money,\\nhits diminishing returns fast, and past a point new bodies slow\\nthe ones already there.\\n\\nFast-tracking: run things in parallel that were supposed to be\\nsequential. Costs no money, adds rework risk — you start plating\\nbefore the sauce is checked, and sometimes it isn't right.\\n\\nCrashing buys time with money. Fast-tracking buys time with risk.\\n\\nEvery rushed open uses one of them. Worth knowing which you just\\nchose.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 942,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-11\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C16\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 16,\n    \"title\": \"Scope\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The feature list I started with had 31 items. What shipped had 6.\\n\\nNot because the other 25 were bad. Because a product that does\\nsix things well is testable, explainable, and finishable, and a\\nproduct that does 31 things is a description of the next four\\nyears.\\n\\nThe discipline is asking what has to be true for the thing to be\\nuseful at all, and refusing everything that only makes it better.\\n\\nBetter is the enemy here. Useful is the bar.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 270,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-11\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B11\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 11,\n    \"title\": \"Regular rate\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Overtime isn't 1.5x the hourly rate. It's 1.5x the regular rate,\\nand those are different numbers more often than people think.\\n\\nNondiscretionary bonuses — attendance bonuses, production\\nbonuses, anything promised in advance to drive behavior — have\\nto be folded into the regular rate before the overtime multiplier\\nis applied.\\n\\nPay a $200 monthly attendance bonus and you may owe additional\\novertime on every OT hour that month.\\n\\nThe bonus everyone thought was generous is frequently the thing\\nthat creates the violation.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 174,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-14\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A17\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 17,\n    \"title\": \"Prime cost\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Food cost is the number everyone watches and it's the wrong one\\nto watch alone.\\n\\nPrime cost is food plus labor together. Around 60% to 65% of\\nsales is the range most full-service operations aim for, and the\\ntwo halves trade against each other constantly.\\n\\nBuy pre-cut produce: food cost up, prep labor down.\\nButcher in house: food cost down, labor up.\\n\\nManage them separately and you'll win one and lose the other\\nwithout noticing you broke even.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 248,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-14\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A46\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 46,\n    \"title\": \"Value innovation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The interesting move isn't beating your competitors. It's not\\nbeing compared to them.\\n\\nTake the standard package in your category and ask which parts\\nyou can cut hard, which you can raise hard, and what nobody in\\nyour segment offers at all.\\n\\nCut table service, raise ingredient quality, add speed — that's\\na whole category, and it wasn't invented by winning the old\\ncomparison.\\n\\nEveryone competing on the same handful of variables is a signal.\\nThe variables are the thing to question.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 708,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-15\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C9\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 9,\n    \"title\": \"Pricing\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Pricing per user is wrong for restaurant software and it took me\\ntoo long to see why.\\n\\nTurnover is the whole industry. If the price goes up every time\\nsomeone new is hired, the product punishes the operator for the\\nthing they can't control, in the month it hurts most.\\n\\nPer location is closer. It's predictable, it survives a bad\\nquarter, and it doesn't create a reason to leave people out of\\ntraining.\\n\\nPricing isn't a number. It's a behavior you're choosing to\\nencourage, and the per-seat version encourages under-training.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 143,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-15\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B40\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 40,\n    \"title\": \"Background checks\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"If you run background checks through a third party, the FCRA\\napplies to you, and it has a sequence.\\n\\nStandalone written disclosure — its own document, not buried in\\nthe application. Written authorization. Then, before you act on\\nanything adverse, a pre-adverse action notice with a copy of the\\nreport and a summary of rights, a reasonable window to respond,\\nand only then the final notice.\\n\\nThe most common failure is the first step: putting the\\ndisclosure inside the job application. That alone has driven\\nclass actions.\\n\\nThe process is free. Skipping it isn't.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 685,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D7\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 7,\n    \"title\": \"Interleaving\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Practicing one thing until you've got it is the most satisfying\\nway to study and one of the weakest.\\n\\nBlocked practice — twenty of the same problem type — produces\\nfast, confident performance in the session and poor performance\\nlater.\\n\\nMixing types up feels clumsy and slow. You keep having to work\\nout which approach applies, which is exactly the skill that\\ntransfers, because real problems don't arrive labeled.\\n\\nPerformance during practice and learning from practice are\\ndifferent things, and they frequently point opposite directions.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 100,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B24\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 24,\n    \"title\": \"Harassment standard\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A hostile work environment claim generally requires conduct that\\nis severe OR pervasive. Not both.\\n\\nThat \\\"or\\\" does a lot of work.\\n\\nOne incident can qualify if it's serious enough. A steady drip of\\nsmaller conduct can qualify without any single instance being\\ndramatic.\\n\\nKitchens run on a culture of sharp talk, and the honest version\\nof this is that the pervasive branch is where the industry's\\nexposure actually sits.\\n\\nNobody gets there through one Saturday. They get there through\\neight months of Saturdays.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 404,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-17\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A3\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Sunk cost\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The walk-in is full of product you over-ordered.\\n\\nThat money is gone. It is gone whether you run the special\\nor not. It cannot be recovered by any decision you make today.\\n\\nSo the only real question is: does running this special make\\nmore money than running something else tonight?\\n\\nIf the answer is no, the full walk-in is not a reason. That's\\na sunk cost, and it's the most expensive kind of thinking in\\nany kitchen.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 35,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-17\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A32\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 32,\n    \"title\": \"Herzberg\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Fixing what people complain about will not make them happy. It\\nwill make them stop complaining.\\n\\nTwo different sets of factors. Hygiene factors — pay, schedule,\\nconditions, the broken AC — cause dissatisfaction when they're\\nbad. Fixing them gets you to neutral.\\n\\nMotivators — responsibility, growth, recognition, real work —\\nare what actually produce satisfaction.\\n\\nFix the AC. You have to. Just don't expect gratitude, because\\nworking AC was never a gift.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 480,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-18\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D9\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 9,\n    \"title\": \"Illusion of competence\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Highlighting a textbook produces a beautifully marked book and\\nalmost no memory.\\n\\nThe mechanism is that recognition is easy and feels like\\nknowing. You see the yellow line, the sentence looks familiar,\\nyour brain returns \\\"yes, I know this.\\\"\\n\\nYou don't know it. You recognize it. Those are separate\\ncapacities and only one of them shows up on an exam or on a\\nshift.\\n\\nTest: close the book and write the argument from memory. The gap\\nbetween how well you thought that would go and how it actually\\ngoes is the illusion, measured.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 136,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-18\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A61\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 61,\n    \"title\": \"Scope creep\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Nobody ever approved the menu getting to 74 items. It got there\\none reasonable request at a time.\\n\\nScope creep is uncontrolled expansion without corresponding\\nadjustments to time, cost, or people. Each addition is small,\\ndefensible, and requested by someone who matters.\\n\\nThe cost never shows up on the item. It shows up in prep hours,\\nwalk-in space, training time, and ticket times four months later.\\n\\nThe discipline isn't refusing additions. It's requiring that\\nsomething comes off when something goes on.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 960,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-21\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C2\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Founder hours\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The largest cost in my business doesn't appear in any ledger.\\n\\nIt's my own hours. And because I don't invoice myself, the\\nwhole venture looks far cheaper than it is.\\n\\nOpportunity cost says the real price of an hour is the value\\nof the next best use of it. For me that's a knowable wage.\\n\\nNot counting it is how people work on something for three\\nyears without ever finding out whether the economics close.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 24,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-21\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B8\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 8,\n    \"title\": \"Complaint procedure\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A functioning complaint process isn't HR paperwork. It's a\\nlegal asset.\\n\\nWhere no tangible employment action occurred, an employer may\\nhave a defense if it exercised reasonable care to prevent and\\ncorrect harassment and the employee unreasonably failed to use\\nthe complaint process.\\n\\nThat defense requires the process to actually exist and\\nactually work. A policy nobody has read protects nobody.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 127,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-22\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C31\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 31,\n    \"title\": \"Naming\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I named the company before I understood the business, and I'd do\\nit differently now.\\n\\nA name should either say what the thing does or be empty enough\\nto fill with meaning later. The bad middle is a name that implies\\nsomething adjacent to what you actually sell.\\n\\nMine leans hospitality, which is right, and leans upscale, which\\nisn't quite where the product lives.\\n\\nNot worth changing at this stage — the switching cost is real and\\nthe name is not the constraint.\\n\\nBut it's a live example of a decision made early on thin\\ninformation that you carry for a long time.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 541,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-22\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B37\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 37,\n    \"title\": \"Arbitration\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"An arbitration agreement in your onboarding packet does less than\\nwhoever sold it to you implied.\\n\\nThey're broadly enforceable, and they do keep most individual\\nclaims out of court and out of class actions.\\n\\nBut sexual assault and sexual harassment claims can no longer be\\nforced into arbitration by a pre-dispute agreement — federal law\\nchanged that in 2022, and the employee chooses.\\n\\nArbitration also isn't cheap. In many consumer and employment\\nframeworks the employer carries most of the forum cost, which\\nchanges the math on small claims considerably.\\n\\nIt's a real tool. It just isn't the wall it gets sold as.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 631,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A18\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 18,\n    \"title\": \"Relevant range\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"Fixed cost\\\" has an expiration date and nobody mentions it.\\n\\nYour rent is fixed — until you add covers you can't seat and\\nneed a second location. Your salaried manager is fixed — until\\nvolume needs two of them.\\n\\nCosts are only fixed inside a relevant range of activity. Push\\npast it and the fixed cost steps up all at once.\\n\\nGrowth doesn't hurt because of the food. It hurts because of the\\nstep you didn't see coming.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 264,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A47\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 47,\n    \"title\": \"Make or buy\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"We make it in house\\\" is a strategy decision that mostly gets\\nmade by pride.\\n\\nMake it yourself when it's genuinely better, when it's what\\nyou're known for, or when doing it yourself teaches you\\nsomething you need.\\n\\nBuy it when a supplier does it cheaper at the same quality and\\nthe customer can't tell.\\n\\nHouse-made bread when it's the thing people mention: correct.\\nHouse-made bread because buying it feels like giving up: that's\\nforty labor hours a week bought with feelings.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 725,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-24\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D11\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 11,\n    \"title\": \"Explaining\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The fastest way to find the holes in what you know is to try to\\nexplain it to someone who doesn't.\\n\\nNot summarize. Explain — in plain words, without the jargon,\\nanswering the follow-up questions.\\n\\nJargon is where the gaps hide. As long as you can say\\n\\\"contribution margin,\\\" you never have to demonstrate that you\\nknow what it is. Take the phrase away and you either understand\\nit or you stall.\\n\\nEvery time I've stalled explaining something to a cook, the\\nstall was the real state of my knowledge and the fluency was\\nthe illusion.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 172,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-24\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A4\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Expectancy theory\",\n    \"figure\": 9,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": \"attach figure 9\",\n    \"body\": \"Your best cook stopped caring. Here's the actual diagnosis.\\n\\nMotivation is three things multiplied together:\\n\\nCan my effort actually produce good performance?\\nWill good performance actually get recognized?\\nDo I want what recognition gets me?\\n\\nMultiplied. Not added.\\n\\nA zero anywhere makes the whole thing zero. Which is why the\\ncook who's been passed over twice works at exactly the speed\\nrequired and not one step faster. He didn't get lazy. One of\\nthose three went to zero and nobody checked which.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 50,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-25\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C24\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 24,\n    \"title\": \"Records\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The feature I almost cut is the one operators respond to first.\\n\\nNot the training. The record that the training happened — who\\ncompleted what, when, signed off by whom, exportable.\\n\\nFor a cook it's paperwork. For an operator it's the difference\\nbetween \\\"we train our people\\\" and being able to prove it, and the\\nsecond one has value the first one doesn't.\\n\\nCompliance features feel unglamorous when you're building. They\\nturn out to be a lot of why anyone buys.\\n\\nThe person paying has a different problem than the person using.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 412,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-25\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B21\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 21,\n    \"title\": \"Joint employment\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Franchisees get sued alongside franchisors, and the question is\\nnever what the franchise agreement says.\\n\\nJoint employer analysis looks at who actually controls the terms\\nand conditions of employment — hiring, firing, supervision,\\nscheduling, pay, and the manner of work.\\n\\nThe standard has moved several times in the last decade and will\\nmove again.\\n\\nWhich is exactly why the franchise agreement isn't the shield\\noperators think it is. What matters is who is really running the\\nschedule.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 351,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-28\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A33\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 33,\n    \"title\": \"Job characteristics\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Most kitchen jobs are demotivating by design, and none of it was\\ndeliberate.\\n\\nFive things make work feel meaningful: variety of skill, doing a\\nwhole task instead of a fragment, work that visibly matters,\\nautonomy over how it's done, and feedback from the work itself.\\n\\nNow picture the person who portions the same protein for six\\nhours and never sees a plate leave.\\n\\nYou didn't hire someone with a bad attitude. You built a station\\nwith four of five missing.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 496,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-28\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A62\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 62,\n    \"title\": \"Earned value\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"We're on schedule and on budget\\\" can both be true while a\\nproject is failing.\\n\\nEarned value asks a third question: how much of the work is\\nactually done? Compare what you planned to spend by now, what\\nyou've actually spent, and what you've genuinely completed.\\n\\nHalfway through the remodel budget with a third of the work\\nfinished is not \\\"on budget.\\\" It's a 33% overrun that hasn't\\nbeen billed yet.\\n\\nSpend tells you about the past. Completion tells you where you'll\\nland.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 976,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-29\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C17\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 17,\n    \"title\": \"Distribution\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I can build faster than I can reach anyone, and that ratio is the\\nactual constraint on this business.\\n\\nThe instinct when the numbers are quiet is to build more. It's\\nfamiliar, it's measurable, it feels like progress, and I'm good\\nat it.\\n\\nBut another feature doesn't help a product nobody has seen. The\\nbottleneck is upstream of the code and has been for a while.\\n\\nWhich is most of the reason this account exists. Writing in\\npublic is the cheapest distribution available to someone with no\\nbudget and a real opinion.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 285,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-29\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B5\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Concerted activity\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Two cooks discussing their pay in the walk-in are engaged in\\nprotected concerted activity under Section 7 of the NLRA.\\n\\nNo union required. It protects unrepresented employees too.\\n\\nPolicies telling staff not to discuss wages are on very old,\\nvery shaky ground, and a lot of handbooks still contain them.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 91,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D13\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 13,\n    \"title\": \"Practice vs experience\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Fifteen years of experience and one year repeated fifteen times\\nlook identical on a resume.\\n\\nThe difference is whether the work included attempts at things\\njust past your current ability, with feedback fast enough to\\ncorrect.\\n\\nMost jobs stop supplying that around year two. You reach\\ncompetent, the work stops being hard, and the clock keeps\\nrunning.\\n\\nTime is not the input. Attempted difficulty with feedback is.\\n\\nWhich is why the person who's been there four years is sometimes\\nbetter than the person who's been there twelve, and why that\\nisn't unfair.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 207,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-09-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B34\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 34,\n    \"title\": \"Safety retaliation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"An employee who refuses to work over a safety concern may be\\nprotected even if they turn out to be wrong.\\n\\nSection 11(c) protects employees who report hazards or exercise\\nrights under the Act. And where an employee reasonably believes\\nthere's a real danger of death or serious harm, there's no time\\nto get it inspected, and they've asked the employer to fix it,\\na refusal can be protected.\\n\\nReasonable belief. Not correctness.\\n\\nWhich means the disciplinary write-up you're drafting has a\\nsecond audience you weren't planning for.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 577,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-01\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A19\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 19,\n    \"title\": \"Step costs\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The 41st cover an hour can cost you $600 a week.\\n\\nNot because of food. Because it's the cover that means you need\\nanother server on the floor, and that person costs the same\\nwhether they run four tables or one.\\n\\nLabor doesn't scale smoothly. It scales in bodies.\\n\\nWhich is why the schedule is a series of cliffs, not a slope, and\\nwhy \\\"just add a little more volume\\\" isn't always the win it looks\\nlike on paper.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 279,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-01\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A48\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 48,\n    \"title\": \"First mover disadvantage\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Being first to a concept in your market is often a bad hand.\\n\\nYou pay for the education. You explain what the thing is, train\\ncustomers to want it, make the expensive mistakes, and discover\\nwhich parts don't work.\\n\\nThe second entrant reads all of that for free and opens a\\ncleaner version across the street.\\n\\nFirst mover advantage is real, but it needs something to lock it\\nin — a location, a supply relationship, a brand people already\\nattached to. Without one of those, being early is just paying\\ntuition for the competition.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 742,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-02\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D15\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 15,\n    \"title\": \"Worked examples\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"For a genuine beginner, being handed a problem to struggle with\\nis worse than being shown a finished solution.\\n\\nEarly on, working memory is fully consumed just holding the\\npieces. There's nothing left to notice the pattern with.\\n\\nA worked example — the whole thing, done, with the reasoning\\nvisible — teaches faster. Then you fade the support: half done,\\nthen the last step only, then nothing.\\n\\nStruggle is productive after a foundation exists. Before that\\nit's just load.\\n\\nMost on-the-job training gets the order exactly backwards.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 243,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-02\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A5\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Variance analysis\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your food cost came in under budget. That might be bad news.\\n\\nCost variance splits into two parts: price and quantity.\\n\\nYou paid less per case — favorable price variance. But if you\\nbought lower-grade produce, you'll see it in waste, in prep\\ntime, and in plates coming back.\\n\\n\\\"Favorable\\\" means different from standard. It does not mean good.\\nChasing favorable price variance is how kitchens quietly wreck\\ntheir own food.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 68,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-05\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C10\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 10,\n    \"title\": \"Removing my own metrics\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I deleted the traction numbers from my own pitch materials\\nbecause I couldn't defend how they were produced.\\n\\nThey weren't invented out of nothing. They were the kind of\\nprojection that gets built to fill a slide and then quietly\\nbecomes a claim.\\n\\nWhat replaced them is a description of what the system does and\\nwhat it's built to do. Capability, not results.\\n\\nIt's a weaker slide. It's also a slide I can be asked about.\\n\\nAn operator who catches one soft number stops believing the\\nother nine, and they should.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 160,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-05\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B18\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 18,\n    \"title\": \"Minors\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The rules for a 16-year-old and a 17-year-old in your kitchen are\\nnot the same, and the equipment is where operators get caught.\\n\\nFederal hazardous occupation orders restrict minors under 18 from\\noperating power-driven meat slicers, grinders, and certain mixers\\nand bakery machines — including cleaning or setting them up.\\n\\nUnder 16, the restrictions widen a lot: cooking is limited,\\nbaking is generally out, and hours are capped by school schedule.\\n\\nEvery state layers its own rules on top, and several are\\nstricter.\\n\\nThe slicer is the one. It gets more kitchens cited than anything\\nelse on the list.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 295,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-06\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C3\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Five Forces\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I analyzed my competition and found my biggest competitor is\\n\\\"nothing at all.\\\"\\n\\nNot other software. A laminated binder, a shift lead pointing\\nat a station, and most often no training program whatsoever.\\n\\nNothing is free, already installed, and has no procurement\\nprocess. It's the market leader in my category by a wide\\nmargin.\\n\\nIf you can't beat nothing, you don't have a product.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 38,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-06\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B2\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Tip pools\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Supervisors and managers cannot receive money from a tip pool.\\n\\nNot \\\"shouldn't.\\\" Cannot.\\n\\nThey can keep tips a guest hands them for a table they personally\\nserved. What they cannot do is take a share of everyone else's.\\n\\nAnd if the person you call a shift lead has real authority over\\nscheduling, discipline, or hiring, a regulator may treat them as a\\nsupervisor regardless of what the title says.\\n\\nThe title is not the test. The duties are.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 40,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A34\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 34,\n    \"title\": \"Social loafing\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Effort per person drops as the group gets bigger, and the drop is\\nalmost never conscious.\\n\\nWhen contribution can't be identified individually, output falls.\\nIt's one of the oldest findings in the field and it shows up on\\nevery closing crew that \\\"cleaned together.\\\"\\n\\nThe fix isn't a speech. It's identifiability.\\n\\nAssign the walk-in to a person, not to the shift. Same crew, same\\nhours, different result, and nobody had to be told to care more.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 512,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A63\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 63,\n    \"title\": \"Three-point estimating\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Ask a cook how long the prep takes and you'll get one number.\\nAsk for three and you'll get the truth.\\n\\nBest case, most likely, worst case. Weight the middle heavily,\\naverage them, and you get an estimate that includes the reality\\nthat things go wrong.\\n\\nSingle-point estimates are always optimistic. Not because people\\nlie, but because they picture the run where nothing breaks.\\n\\nThree numbers takes ten extra seconds and it's the difference\\nbetween a schedule and a wish.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 993,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-08\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D17\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 17,\n    \"title\": \"Forgetting curve\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"You will lose most of what you learned today within a week, and\\nthe schedule of that loss is predictable enough to plan around.\\n\\nThe drop is steep at first and then flattens. Which means the\\ntiming of review matters far more than the amount.\\n\\nA five-minute review at day one, day three, and day seven beats\\nan hour at day thirty, easily.\\n\\nPractical version: whatever you learned today, look at it again\\ntomorrow for two minutes. That single habit does more than any\\nstudy technique I've tried.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 279,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-08\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A20\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 20,\n    \"title\": \"Profit vs cash flow\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Profit is what you earned. Cash is what you can spend. They are\\nnot the same number and they don't arrive at the same time.\\n\\nYou booked the wedding — that's profit in the month you serve it.\\nYou bought the product — that's cash out in the week you ordered.\\nYou paid the crew — cash out on Friday.\\n\\nEvery business that fails while profitable fails in that gap.\\n\\nWatch both. If you only get to watch one, watch the cash.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 294,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C32\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 32,\n    \"title\": \"A competitor raised\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A better-funded competitor entering your category is not\\nautomatically bad news, and the reflex to panic skips a step.\\n\\nFunding buys them market education. They will spend money\\nteaching operators that this category exists and that the problem\\nis worth solving — which is the expensive part, and I'd be\\npaying it myself otherwise.\\n\\nWhat it doesn't buy is the thing they'd need to beat me on\\ncontent, which is time on a line.\\n\\nTheir money is a real advantage. So is the fact that they'll open\\nthe door I couldn't afford to open.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 560,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B31\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 31,\n    \"title\": \"I-9\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The I-9 mistakes that get penalized are almost never about who\\nwas hired. They're about paperwork.\\n\\nSection 1 by the first day of work. Section 2 within three\\nbusiness days. You cannot specify which documents an employee\\npresents, and asking for more than required is its own violation.\\n\\nOver-documentation is a real claim. So is re-verifying a\\npermanent resident card that doesn't need it.\\n\\nFines are per form. A restaurant with sixty hires a year and a\\nsloppy binder is carrying a number most owners have never\\nestimated.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 526,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-12\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A49\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 49,\n    \"title\": \"Segmentation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"Everyone\\\" is not a market and it's the most expensive answer to\\ngive.\\n\\nSegment on behavior, not demographics. The useful groups are\\nthings like weekday lunch under 45 minutes, Friday date night,\\nand the family that needs to be out before bedtime.\\n\\nSame person can be in all three on different days. The demo tells\\nyou nothing. The occasion tells you everything.\\n\\nMenu, pacing, staffing, and pricing should be answering one of\\nthose. Trying to answer all three at once is what a confused\\ndining room feels like from the inside.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 759,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-12\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A6\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"The constrained resource\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your menu is not limited by ingredients. It's limited by the\\nfryer.\\n\\nWhen one station is the bottleneck, the item to push isn't the\\none with the highest margin. It's the one with the highest\\nmargin per minute of the bottleneck.\\n\\nA $19 plate that ties up the fryer for four minutes loses to a\\n$14 plate that takes ninety seconds. Every time. All night.\\n\\nMargin per plate is the wrong denominator when something is\\nconstrained.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 83,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-13\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C25\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 25,\n    \"title\": \"Decaying advantage\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The thing that makes me credible in this market has a shelf life\\nand the clock is already running.\\n\\nFifteen years of back-of-house experience is real. It's also\\nfrozen the day I stop working the line, and it depreciates from\\nthere. Equipment changes, labor conditions change, the job\\nchanges.\\n\\nRight now I'm current because I'm still on the schedule. That\\nwon't last.\\n\\nSo the question isn't how to protect the advantage. It's what to\\nconvert it into before it decays — written material, a system,\\nrelationships, a product that encodes it.\\n\\nAdvantages you don't convert are advantages you spend.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 429,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-13\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B15\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 15,\n    \"title\": \"Travel time\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Sending a cook to your other location mid-shift is paid time.\\nTheir drive to work in the morning isn't.\\n\\nOrdinary home-to-work commuting isn't compensable. But travel\\nbetween job sites during the workday generally is, and so is\\ntravel that's part of the job's principal activity.\\n\\nThe run to the restaurant supply store. The shift split across\\ntwo units. The manager covering another location for four hours.\\n\\nIf you're moving people between buildings, that's not commuting.\\nThat's the workday.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 244,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-14\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D19\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 19,\n    \"title\": \"Base rates\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Before you ask how good this specific opportunity looks, ask how\\noften things in this category work at all.\\n\\nVivid, specific information overwhelms boring statistical\\ninformation almost every time. A compelling story about one\\nrestaurant that made it will beat the closure rate for the whole\\ncategory, in your head, without your permission.\\n\\nThe base rate isn't the answer. It's the starting point that the\\nspecifics should move you away from.\\n\\nMost people skip straight to the specifics and never set the\\nstarting point at all.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 312,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-14\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B44\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 44,\n    \"title\": \"Predictive scheduling\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"In a growing number of cities and states, changing the schedule\\nafter you post it costs money.\\n\\nFair workweek laws typically require advance notice — often 14\\ndays — and require predictability pay when you change, cancel, or\\nadd a shift inside the window. Some require rest between closing\\nand opening shifts, and offering hours to existing staff before\\nhiring.\\n\\nThey're local. Most of the country has nothing. A handful of\\njurisdictions have all of it.\\n\\nIf you operate in one and don't know it, your standard practice\\nof texting the Friday change on Wednesday is a per-shift penalty.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 758,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-15\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A35\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 35,\n    \"title\": \"Psychological safety\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The strongest predictor of whether your kitchen learns from\\nmistakes is whether people can admit them without cost.\\n\\nPsychological safety isn't comfort or niceness. It's the shared\\nbelief that saying \\\"I dropped it\\\" or \\\"I don't know this station\\\"\\nwon't be used against you.\\n\\nLow-safety kitchens don't have fewer errors. They have fewer\\nreported errors, which is much more expensive.\\n\\nYou'll know which one you run by what people tell you at 9pm on\\na bad Saturday.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 527,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-15\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A64\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 64,\n    \"title\": \"Student syndrome\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Give a prep list four hours of slack and it will take four hours\\nof slack.\\n\\nWork expands to fill the time available, and people start at the\\nlast responsible moment. Both effects are well documented and\\nboth are running in your kitchen right now.\\n\\nWhich is why padding every task is self-defeating. The buffer\\ngets consumed before the risk it was protecting against arrives.\\n\\nPool the buffer at the end instead of hiding it in each task.\\nSame total time, and it's actually there when something goes\\nwrong.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1009,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D21\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 21,\n    \"title\": \"Goodhart's law\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every metric you attach a consequence to gets gamed, and usually\\nby good people.\\n\\nMeasure ticket times and food starts leaving the window before\\nit's ready. Measure food cost and quality quietly drops.\\nMeasure covers and nobody wants the four-top that lingers.\\n\\nNot corruption. The measure became the goal, and it was always a\\npartial proxy for the goal.\\n\\nYou can't solve this by finding a better measure. You solve it\\nby using several, changing them occasionally, and keeping\\njudgment in the loop.\\n\\nAnyone promising you the one right number is selling something.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 347,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A21\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 21,\n    \"title\": \"Time value of money\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A dollar this Friday genuinely beats a dollar next quarter, and\\nnot because of inflation.\\n\\nThat dollar can buy product that becomes four dollars of covers\\nthis weekend. Money has a job it could be doing, and time it\\nspends not doing that job is a real cost.\\n\\nThis is why a vendor offering 2% off for paying in ten days\\ninstead of thirty is a much bigger deal than 2% sounds.\\n\\nRun the annualized number sometime. It's not close.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 308,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-19\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C18\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 18,\n    \"title\": \"CAC\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The customer acquisition cost that works for my product is\\nroughly zero, and that fact determined my entire strategy.\\n\\nRun the arithmetic. A modest monthly price against a realistic\\nretention period gives you a lifetime value. Whatever fraction of\\nthat you can spend to acquire a customer is your budget.\\n\\nMine is small enough that paid acquisition is off the table\\nentirely.\\n\\nSo the channels have to be ones where the cost is my time rather\\nthan my money: writing, replies, direct conversations, and\\nwhatever comes from someone reading six things and deciding I'm\\nworth an email.\\n\\nConstraint first, strategy second. Not the other way around.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 302,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-19\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B28\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 28,\n    \"title\": \"PWFA\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The Pregnant Workers Fairness Act is newer than most restaurant\\nhandbooks and it changed the default.\\n\\nFor covered employers — 15 or more employees — it requires\\nreasonable accommodation for known limitations related to\\npregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions, absent\\nundue hardship.\\n\\nThe practical list is small and cheap: a stool, more bathroom\\nbreaks, water on the line, a temporary lifting limit, schedule\\nadjustment.\\n\\nIt doesn't require a disability. That's the part people get\\nwrong — the old analysis asked whether the condition was severe\\nenough. This one doesn't.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 474,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-20\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C11\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 11,\n    \"title\": \"Authorization\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"My product is finished enough to pilot and I haven't deployed it\\nwhere I work.\\n\\nBecause I haven't got authorization, and building something that\\nwould be useful to an employer doesn't give you the right to\\ninstall it in their operation.\\n\\nThis is slower. It's cost me months of the exact evidence I need\\nmost.\\n\\nIt's also the whole difference between a founder with a pilot and\\na former employee with a legal problem. The version of me that\\nskips this step gets one deployment and no career.\\n\\nWait for the yes.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 178,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-20\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B12\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 12,\n    \"title\": \"The 80/20 rule is gone\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"If you're still training managers on the 80/20 tip credit rule,\\nyou're teaching a regulation that no longer exists.\\n\\nThe DOL's 80/20/30 rule was vacated by the Fifth Circuit in 2024\\nand the department withdrew it.\\n\\nThat doesn't mean side work is unlimited. The older dual jobs\\nconcept is still there — an employee working a genuinely\\nseparate non-tipped job is owed full minimum wage for that time.\\n\\nAnd states set their own rules on top, some far stricter than\\nfederal.\\n\\nThe federal quantitative test went away. The exposure didn't.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 191,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-21\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A50\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 50,\n    \"title\": \"Anchoring\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Put one expensive item on the menu that almost nobody orders. It\\nearns its space anyway.\\n\\nThe $58 tomahawk isn't there to sell. It's there so the $34 ribeye\\nreads as reasonable.\\n\\nPeople don't evaluate prices in isolation. They evaluate them\\nagainst whatever else is in view, and you control what's in view.\\n\\nThis is real and it's well documented. It also stops working the\\nmoment the anchor looks absurd, which is where menu design turns\\nback into judgment.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 776,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-21\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A7\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 7,\n    \"title\": \"Cash conversion cycle\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"An underrated fact about restaurants: the cash cycle is\\nalmost free money.\\n\\nMost businesses buy inventory, wait 60 days to sell it, then\\nwait another 45 to get paid. That gap has to be financed.\\n\\nYou buy on Tuesday, sell on Friday, get paid instantly, and\\npay your vendor in 30 days.\\n\\nYou're operating on your suppliers' money. That's a real\\nstructural advantage and almost nobody in the industry says\\nit out loud.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 99,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-22\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D23\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 23,\n    \"title\": \"Inversion\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Instead of asking how to make this work, ask what would\\nguarantee it fails. Then don't do those.\\n\\nIt's a genuinely different question and the answers arrive\\nfaster.\\n\\nHow do I build a great training program is vague. What would\\nmake a training program fail is specific and immediate: nobody\\nhas time for it, the content is generic, there's no record of\\ncompletion, and the managers don't use it themselves.\\n\\nAvoiding the known failures gets you most of the way there, and\\nthe failures are much easier to enumerate than the successes.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 386,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-22\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A36\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 36,\n    \"title\": \"Attribution error\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"When the new guy is slow, you think he's slow. When you're slow,\\nyou think the ticket printer jammed.\\n\\nFundamental attribution error: we explain other people with\\ncharacter and ourselves with circumstance.\\n\\nIt matters because you staff and fire on those explanations.\\n\\nBefore you conclude someone is lazy, check whether they've been\\nhanded a station with a broken low-boy and no par sheet. It costs\\nyou ten seconds and it's right often enough to change decisions.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 543,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C4\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Pilot design\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A pilot that can't tell a real effect from an accident\\nproduces a testimonial, not evidence.\\n\\nThree rules I'm holding myself to:\\n\\nDefine the measure before it starts, not after you see which\\nnumber looks good.\\n\\nGet a comparison. A group improving over time proves nothing —\\nnew hires get faster anyway.\\n\\nSay the confound out loud. I'd be running this where I work,\\nso my presence is a plausible cause of any improvement. Better\\nI name that than the buyer does.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 53,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B41\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 41,\n    \"title\": \"Handbooks\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A handbook can create the contract you wrote it to avoid.\\n\\nLanguage that promises progressive discipline, guarantees process\\nbefore termination, or lists the only reasons someone can be let\\ngo has been read by courts as contractual in a number of states.\\n\\nThe fix is boring: a clear at-will statement, an acknowledgment\\nsigned at hire, discretionary language throughout, and a stated\\nright to modify.\\n\\nThen actually follow what's left. A handbook you don't follow is\\nworse than the one you don't have, because now the deviation is\\ndocumented.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 703,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-26\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A65\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 65,\n    \"title\": \"Risk register\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every operator carries a list of things that could go wrong. Almost\\nnone of them write it down, which is why the list resets every\\ntime a manager leaves.\\n\\nA register is four columns: what could happen, how likely, how bad,\\nand what we'd do.\\n\\nWalk-in compressor. Key opener quits. Health inspection during a\\nremodel. Delivery truck doesn't show Friday.\\n\\nNone of that is new information to you. Writing it down is what\\nturns it from your intuition into the operation's property.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1026,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-26\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A22\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 22,\n    \"title\": \"Net present value\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"It pays for itself in two years\\\" is not an analysis.\\n\\nNPV asks a harder question: if I take every dollar this thing\\nwill earn, discount each one for how long I have to wait, and\\nsubtract what it costs today — is what's left positive?\\n\\nThe discount is the point. A dollar in year four is worth less\\nthan a dollar in year one, so a project that's back-loaded is\\nworth less than the same total spread evenly.\\n\\nTwo projects can return identical totals and only one is worth\\ndoing.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 323,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-27\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C33\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 33,\n    \"title\": \"Time to value\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The only metric I'd keep if I had to drop the rest is how long it\\ntakes a new operator to get one useful thing out of the product.\\n\\nNot signups. Not engagement. Time to the first moment where they\\nthink \\\"oh, that's handy.\\\"\\n\\nEvery day of setup between purchase and that moment is a day the\\ndecision can be reversed, and in an industry where the buyer is\\ninterrupted constantly, a long setup is functionally a refusal.\\n\\nIf it takes two weeks to configure, most people never find out\\nwhether it works.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 577,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-27\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B25\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 25,\n    \"title\": \"Religious accommodation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The bar for refusing a religious scheduling request went up\\nsharply and most operators never got the memo.\\n\\nFor decades employers relied on a de minimis reading — anything\\nmore than trivial cost was enough to refuse.\\n\\nThe Supreme Court rejected that in 2023. The employer now has to\\nshow the burden is substantial in the overall context of the\\nbusiness.\\n\\n\\\"It would be inconvenient to reschedule\\\" was the old answer.\\nIt is not the current one.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 423,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-28\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D25\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 25,\n    \"title\": \"Models\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every framework you learn is wrong. The useful question is what\\nit's wrong about and whether that matters here.\\n\\nFive Forces ignores complements and moves slowly. Supply and\\ndemand assumes information nobody has. Maslow's hierarchy\\ndoesn't hold up as a strict sequence.\\n\\nAll still useful. A model is a deliberate simplification, and\\nthe simplification is the feature — it's what lets you think at\\nall.\\n\\nThe failure isn't using an imperfect model. It's forgetting you\\ndid, and mistaking the map for the thing.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 422,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-28\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B9\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 9,\n    \"title\": \"Off the clock\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"Clock out, then finish your side work\\\" is the single most\\nexpensive sentence a manager says.\\n\\nWork you know about, or should know about, is compensable\\nwhether or not it's on the timecard. Suffered or permitted is\\nthe standard, and it does not require you to have asked.\\n\\nThe fifteen minutes after clock-out. The prep before clock-in.\\nThe manager who \\\"just finishes up.\\\"\\n\\nNone of that is a favor. It's unrecorded wages, and the records\\nare your responsibility to keep, not theirs.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 141,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-29\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A51\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 51,\n    \"title\": \"Product life cycle\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every menu item is somewhere on a curve, and the mistake is\\nmanaging them all the same way.\\n\\nIntroduction: nobody orders it, needs a push.\\nGrowth: sales climbing, protect the supply.\\nMaturity: steady, this is where your money is made.\\nDecline: slipping, and it's taking up a station.\\n\\nThe item people loved four years ago that now sells nine a week\\nisn't a tradition. It's a station slot, a prep task, and three\\nSKUs in the walk-in.\\n\\nMenus don't get bloated by adding. They get bloated by not\\nsubtracting.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 792,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-29\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A8\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 8,\n    \"title\": \"Opportunity cost\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The owner working the line for free is the most expensive\\nlabor in the building.\\n\\nNot because of what it costs. Because of what it prevents.\\n\\nThose are the hours that would have gone to vendor\\nnegotiation, menu engineering, hiring, or getting the second\\nlocation open. None of that shows up on a P&L, which is\\nexactly why it never gets counted.\\n\\nFree labor is only free if the alternative was doing nothing.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 115,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D27\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 27,\n    \"title\": \"Synthesis\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"At the end of anything you learn, write one page from memory with\\nthe source closed.\\n\\nNot a summary of the material. The version you'd give someone\\nwho has to make a decision tomorrow.\\n\\nIt forces three things at once: retrieval, selection of what\\nactually matters, and translation into plain language. Those are\\nthe three operations that convert reading into knowledge.\\n\\nIt takes twenty minutes and it's the only artifact from a course\\nI've ever gone back to.\\n\\nEverything else — highlights, slides, my own notes — I have never\\nopened again. Not once.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 455,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-10-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A37\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 37,\n    \"title\": \"Span of control\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"There's a hard limit on how many people one person can actually\\nsupervise, and it's lower than your schedule assumes.\\n\\nThe number moves with how complex and how variable the work is.\\nSimple, repetitive, stable work — one person can oversee a lot.\\nFast, interdependent, constantly interrupted work — far fewer.\\n\\nA Saturday dinner rush is the second kind.\\n\\nIf your one manager is nominally over twenty-two people during\\npeak, they aren't supervising. They're reacting, and the\\nschedule is what decided that.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 558,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-02\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C26\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 26,\n    \"title\": \"Single point of failure\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I ran a risk analysis on my own company and the top risk is me.\\n\\nNot competition. Not funding. One person holding all the domain\\nknowledge, all the code, all the relationships, and all the\\ncontext, with no documentation good enough for anyone else to\\npick it up.\\n\\nIf I'm out for six weeks, the company is out for six weeks.\\n\\nEvery solo founder knows this and almost none of us do anything\\nabout it, because the mitigation — writing everything down — is\\nthe least urgent task available on any given day.\\n\\nI'm doing it anyway. Slowly, and later than I should have.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 449,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-02\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B38\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 38,\n    \"title\": \"Final paychecks\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Final pay timing is a state question and the penalties are wildly\\nout of proportion to the amount.\\n\\nSome states require payment on the last day for an involuntary\\ntermination. Others allow the next regular payday. Several\\ndistinguish quit from fired.\\n\\nThe penalty in a number of states is a continuing daily wage —\\nwhich is how a $340 final check turns into a four-figure claim\\nover a paperwork delay.\\n\\nLook up your state's number once and put it on the termination\\nchecklist. It's a ten-minute task that never has to be done\\nagain.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 650,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-03\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C19\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 19,\n    \"title\": \"Free\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I keep going back and forth on a free tier and here is the actual\\ntension.\\n\\nFree removes the procurement problem I don't have an answer for.\\nSomebody can just try it, and in an industry with no evaluation\\nprocess that's worth a lot.\\n\\nFree also attracts the operators least likely to implement\\nanything, generates support load from people who will never pay,\\nand teaches the market a price.\\n\\nThe version I keep landing on is time-limited rather than\\nfeature-limited. You get the whole thing, and then you decide.\\n\\nStill not sure. Writing it down mostly to find out if I believe\\nit.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 322,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-03\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B22\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 22,\n    \"title\": \"At-will\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"At-will employment means you can terminate for any reason or no\\nreason. It does not mean any reason.\\n\\nThe exceptions are the whole subject. You can't terminate for a\\nprotected characteristic, for protected activity, for taking\\nprotected leave, for reporting a safety hazard, for filing a\\ncomp claim, or for protected concerted activity.\\n\\nThat's a long list, and it covers most of the situations where\\nsomeone is actually being fired.\\n\\n\\\"We're at-will\\\" is a true sentence that answers almost none of\\nthe questions people use it to answer.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 368,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A66\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 66,\n    \"title\": \"Risk appetite\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Appetite is how much risk you're willing to take on purpose.\\nTolerance is how much you can absorb before something breaks.\\n\\nConfusing them is expensive in both directions.\\n\\nAn operator with a big appetite and thin tolerance opens the\\nsecond location on a thin margin and loses both.\\n\\nAn operator with a big tolerance and no appetite sits on cash for\\nnine years while the lease appreciates around them.\\n\\nThey're different questions. Answer them separately and in that\\norder.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1042,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A23\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 23,\n    \"title\": \"Payback period\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Payback period is the easiest capital rule to use and the easiest\\none to get burned by.\\n\\nIt tells you how long until you're whole. It tells you nothing\\nabout what happens after.\\n\\nA $12,000 machine that pays back in 18 months and dies at month\\n20 loses to one that pays back in 30 months and runs a decade.\\n\\nUse payback to screen. Never use it to decide.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 339,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-05\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D2\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Bloom's\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"If an assignment says \\\"evaluate\\\" and you summarized, you\\ncapped your own grade before you started.\\n\\nRemember, understand, apply, analyze, evaluate, create.\\n\\nUpper-level rubrics are written for the top three. Explaining\\nwhat a framework is scores as description no matter how\\naccurate it is.\\n\\nThe tell: description spends its words on the framework and\\nmentions the company at the end. Analysis spends its words on\\nthe company.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 19,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-05\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A52\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 52,\n    \"title\": \"Lifetime value\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The regular who spends $31 a visit isn't a $31 customer. She's a\\n$4,800 customer, and you've been treating her like the first\\nnumber.\\n\\nLifetime value is what a relationship is worth over its whole\\nrun — spend per visit, times visits per year, times years,\\nadjusted for margin.\\n\\nOnce you've done that arithmetic, comping a $14 entree on a bad\\nnight stops looking like a loss and starts looking like the\\ncheapest retention spending available.\\n\\nThe comp isn't generosity. It's math.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 810,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-06\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C12\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 12,\n    \"title\": \"Buyer is not the user\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The person who benefits from my product is a line cook. The\\nperson who buys it has never worked my product's screens.\\n\\nThose are different people with different problems, and building\\nfor one while selling to the other is where most operational\\nsoftware dies.\\n\\nThe cook wants to stop being handed a station with no\\nexplanation. The operator wants turnover cost down and a training\\nrecord that exists when someone asks for it.\\n\\nSame product, two arguments. If you only build the first one you\\nnever get in the room, and if you only build the second one\\nnobody uses what you sold.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 197,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-06\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B6\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"FMLA threshold\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"FMLA doesn't apply to most independent restaurants.\\n\\nIt requires 50+ employees within 75 miles, plus the employee\\nhaving 12 months and 1,250 hours of service.\\n\\nWorth knowing in both directions: single-unit operators\\noften think they're covered when they aren't, and multi-unit\\noperators often think they aren't when they are.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 102,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A9\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 9,\n    \"title\": \"Break-even in covers\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Stop calculating break-even in dollars. Calculate it in covers.\\n\\nFixed costs divided by contribution margin per cover gives you\\na number your whole team can actually use.\\n\\n\\\"We need $4,180 tonight\\\" means nothing to a line.\\n\\\"We need 143 covers before we're making money\\\" means\\nsomething to everyone.\\n\\nSame math. One of them changes behavior.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 130,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A38\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 38,\n    \"title\": \"Groupthink\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The meeting where everyone agreed is the meeting that should\\nworry you.\\n\\nGroupthink happens when a cohesive team values agreement over\\naccuracy. Doubts stay unspoken, alternatives never get raised,\\nand the group leaves more confident than the evidence supports.\\n\\nRestaurant leadership teams are unusually prone to it. Small,\\ntight, under time pressure, loyal.\\n\\nAsk the newest person first. They haven't learned yet what\\nthey're supposed to say.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 574,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-10\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C5\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Secondary risk\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Risk management concept almost nobody applies: your response\\nto a risk creates new risk.\\n\\nI'm rebuilding my product's architecture. Correct call —\\nthe debt is real.\\n\\nIt also eats the scarcest thing I have, which is my own hours,\\nduring exactly the window when I might need to move fast\\ncommercially.\\n\\nThat doesn't make the rebuild wrong. It makes the sequencing\\na real decision with a real cost instead of a technical detail.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 71,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-10\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B35\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 35,\n    \"title\": \"Unemployment\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Most terminations you'd describe as \\\"for cause\\\" will still result\\nin benefits being paid, and that surprises operators every time.\\n\\nUnemployment misconduct is a narrower standard than performance\\nproblems. Poor performance, inability to keep up, honest\\nmistakes, and being a bad fit generally don't disqualify.\\n\\nWillful disregard of the employer's interest is closer to the\\nline.\\n\\nContesting every claim is a strategy that costs staff hours and\\nusually loses. Documenting the ones that actually meet the\\nstandard is the version that works.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 594,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-11\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D4\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Frameworks as instruments\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every business framework is a question to ask, not a table to\\nfill in.\\n\\nVRIO isn't a grid you reproduce. It's four questions about one\\nspecific resource, and the first \\\"no\\\" is your answer — you stop\\nthere.\\n\\nFive Forces isn't five paragraphs. It's an answer to which\\nsingle force is squeezing your margin, because that's the one\\nstrategy has to address.\\n\\nFilling in the table is how you get a good grade. Asking the\\nquestion is how you get an answer.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 52,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-11\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B19\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 19,\n    \"title\": \"Recordkeeping\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"When time records are missing, the tie doesn't go to the\\nemployer.\\n\\nThe FLSA puts the recordkeeping obligation on you. Where an\\nemployer's records are inadequate, courts have allowed employees\\nto prove hours by reasonable inference — and the burden shifts to\\nthe employer to rebut it.\\n\\nPractical translation: \\\"we don't have records for that period\\\"\\nis not a defense. It's closer to the opposite.\\n\\nThree years of payroll records. Two years of the underlying time\\ndata. Longer in some states.\\n\\nThe records are cheap. The inference is not.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 314,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-12\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A67\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 67,\n    \"title\": \"Secondary risk\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your response to a risk creates a new risk, and the new one is\\nalmost never on the list.\\n\\nWorried about food safety, so you add three checks per shift.\\nNow closing runs long, the crew rushes the last one, and the\\ncheck that mattered gets signed without being done.\\n\\nWorried about turnover, so you raise starting wage. Now your\\ntwo-year people are making new-hire money and you have a\\ndifferent retention problem.\\n\\nThe mitigation isn't wrong. It's just not free, and pretending\\nit is is how a control register turns into fiction.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1059,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-12\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A24\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 24,\n    \"title\": \"Cost of capital\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"There is no such thing as free money in your business, including\\nyour own.\\n\\nIf you put $40,000 of your own savings into the buildout, that\\nmoney had somewhere else to be. Whatever it would have safely\\nearned is now the minimum this restaurant has to clear before\\nyou're actually ahead.\\n\\nThat's your cost of capital, and it applies to owner money\\nexactly like it applies to the bank's.\\n\\nThe bank sends a statement. Your own money doesn't, which is why\\nit's the one people forget to count.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 353,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-13\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D6\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Spacing\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The same four hours of study produce wildly different results\\ndepending only on how you spread them.\\n\\nFour hours the night before: you'll pass and lose most of it.\\nOne hour a week for four weeks: you'll keep it for years.\\n\\nSame time, same material, same person. The only variable is the\\ngaps, and the gaps are doing the work.\\n\\nForgetting a little between sessions is not a problem to solve.\\nIt's the mechanism. Each time you claw it back, it's stored\\nharder.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 84,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-13\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A53\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 53,\n    \"title\": \"Loss leader\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Selling something at a loss on purpose is a legitimate strategy\\nand a very easy one to run badly.\\n\\nIt works when the losing item reliably pulls a basket that pays\\nfor it. Cheap wings that come with a table of drinks.\\n\\nIt fails when people take the loss item and leave, or when it\\ncannibalizes something profitable they'd have ordered anyway.\\n\\nThe test isn't whether the promotion moved. It's whether the\\naverage check on promotion nights beats the average check on\\nnormal ones. That's a different number and most operators never\\npull it.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 827,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C34\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 34,\n    \"title\": \"Selling to the burned\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Most operators I talk to have already been sold restaurant\\nsoftware that didn't work, and that's the actual starting\\nposition.\\n\\nThey're not skeptical of me. They're carrying a specific memory\\nof a system that took four months to roll out, that nobody used,\\nand that they're still paying for.\\n\\nWhich means the persuasive move isn't enthusiasm. It's naming\\nthe failure mode first — here's why this usually doesn't work,\\nhere's what I've done about it, here's how you'd know early if\\nit isn't working.\\n\\nYou can't out-enthusiasm a bad experience. You can acknowledge\\nit.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 593,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B3\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Exempt status\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": \"update — this changed in May 2026\",\n    \"body\": \"The federal salary floor for overtime exemption moved backward\\nthis year and a lot of operators missed it.\\n\\nThe 2024 rule that was raising it got vacated in court, and in\\nMay 2026 the DOL formally dropped it. We are back to the 2019\\nlevels: $684 a week, $35,568 a year.\\n\\nBut salary was never the whole test. Exemption needs a salary\\nbasis AND a duties test.\\n\\nA kitchen manager who mostly cooks is probably not exempt no\\nmatter what the offer letter says or what they're paid.\\n\\nMisclassification settles in back wages, and it settles for\\neveryone in the role — not just the person who complained.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 56,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-17\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C27\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 27,\n    \"title\": \"Documents before code\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I wrote roughly fifty documents before I wrote the version of\\nthis product I intend to sell.\\n\\nTerms, policies, data handling, what the system promises, what it\\nexplicitly doesn't, who owns what.\\n\\nNobody asked me to. It was, on any given morning, obviously not\\nthe highest-value thing I could do.\\n\\nBut the alternative is writing all of it during a deal, under\\ntime pressure, with the buyer's counsel reading over your\\nshoulder and every ambiguity resolving against you.\\n\\nBoring work done early is the cheapest version of that work.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 467,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-17\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B32\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 32,\n    \"title\": \"Texas non-subscribers\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Texas is the one state where an employer can legally opt out of\\nworkers' compensation, and a lot of restaurants do.\\n\\nHere's the trade most operators don't fully price.\\n\\nCarrying comp gets you the exclusive remedy — an injured employee\\nis generally limited to the comp system and can't sue you in tort.\\n\\nOpt out and you lose that. A non-subscriber can be sued directly,\\nand several of the usual defenses are stripped away by statute.\\n\\nOpting out saves premium. What it costs is the ceiling on what a\\nsingle fryer burn can become.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 543,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-18\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A10\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 10,\n    \"title\": \"Tuckman\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"New crew fighting in week three isn't a hiring mistake.\\n\\nTeams go through forming, storming, norming, performing.\\nStorming is a stage, not a malfunction.\\n\\nThe crew that never argues in week three usually isn't\\nharmonious. The conflict just went somewhere you can't see,\\nand it comes back at the worst possible service.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 144,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-18\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A39\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 39,\n    \"title\": \"Equity theory\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"People don't evaluate their pay. They evaluate their ratio\\nagainst someone else's.\\n\\nWhat I put in over what I get out, compared to what she puts in\\nover what she gets out. When those ratios don't match, people\\nrestore balance — usually by lowering their input, because that's\\nthe lever they control.\\n\\nWhich means a raise for one person can reduce output from three.\\n\\nNot because they're petty. Because that's how the comparison\\nworks, and it happens whether or not anyone talks about it.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 590,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-19\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D8\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 8,\n    \"title\": \"Desirable difficulties\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"There's a category of study technique that reliably makes you\\nworse in the moment and better later.\\n\\nSpacing. Interleaving. Testing yourself before you feel ready.\\nMaking yourself generate an answer instead of recognizing one.\\n\\nAll of them slow the session down. All of them raise the error\\nrate while you're doing them. All of them beat the smooth\\nversion on any test given a week later.\\n\\nWhich is a problem, because the smooth version feels like it's\\nworking and the effective version feels like it isn't.\\n\\nYour sense of how well you're learning is the least reliable\\ninstrument in the room.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 117,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-19\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A68\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 68,\n    \"title\": \"Bullwhip effect\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Small swings in your dining room become huge swings at the farm,\\nand each link amplifies the one before it.\\n\\nYou see a busy weekend and order 20% extra. Your distributor sees\\nthat and orders 35% extra. The processor sees that and books 50%.\\n\\nThen the weather turns, your covers normalize, and everyone up\\nthe chain is holding product nobody ordered.\\n\\nThen the correction comes back down as a shortage.\\n\\nThe pattern is called the bullwhip effect and it's a large part of\\nwhy your prices move for reasons nothing to do with you.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1076,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-20\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C20\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 20,\n    \"title\": \"Churn\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"My market has 70%+ annual employee turnover and I've stopped\\ntreating that as a problem to work around.\\n\\nEvery product decision that assumes a stable user base is wrong\\nhere. Onboarding isn't a first-week event, it's the permanent\\ncondition. Personalization has a short payback. Anything\\nrequiring accumulated familiarity fails.\\n\\nBut turnover is also the reason the product exists. A place that\\nnever lost anyone wouldn't need a training system.\\n\\nThe thing that makes the market hard is the thing that makes it a\\nmarket. That took me embarrassingly long.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 342,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-20\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B16\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 16,\n    \"title\": \"On-call\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"Call in at 3 to see if we need you\\\" may or may not be paid time,\\nand the test isn't what you call it.\\n\\nThe question is whether the employee is engaged to wait or\\nwaiting to be engaged. If the restrictions are tight enough that\\nthey can't effectively use the time for themselves, it's\\ngenerally compensable.\\n\\nTen-minute response radius, phone monitored, can't drink, can't\\nleave town: that's engaged to wait.\\n\\n\\\"We'll text you if it gets busy\\\" usually isn't.\\n\\nThe more control you keep, the more likely you're buying the\\ntime.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 260,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A25\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 25,\n    \"title\": \"Financial leverage\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Debt makes a good year better and a bad year fatal. That's the\\nwhole trade.\\n\\nBorrow to open the second location and you keep all the upside\\nabove the payment. That's leverage working.\\n\\nBut the payment is fixed, and it doesn't move when a highway\\nproject closes your access road for five months.\\n\\nOperating leverage already made your cost structure rigid.\\nFinancial leverage stacks a second rigid layer on top of it.\\n\\nTwo rigid structures and one soft season is the standard story.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 370,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A54\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 54,\n    \"title\": \"Brand equity\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Brand isn't your logo. It's the price premium you can charge for\\na plate the guest can't otherwise distinguish.\\n\\nIf you can sell the same burger for four dollars more than the\\nplace with the same burger, that four dollars is brand equity and\\nit's a real asset on a balance sheet you don't keep.\\n\\nIt's built slowly through consistency and destroyed quickly\\nthrough variance.\\n\\nWhich means the shift where you ran out of the thing you're known\\nfor cost you more than that shift's tickets.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 844,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-24\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C13\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 13,\n    \"title\": \"Procurement\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The hardest part of selling to restaurants isn't convincing\\nanyone. It's that there's no process to say yes.\\n\\nEnterprise software has procurement — a path, a budget line, a\\nperson whose job includes evaluating this.\\n\\nAn independent operator has a GM who is on the floor, an owner\\nwho is at another location, and no calendar slot that means\\n\\\"evaluate a new system.\\\"\\n\\nWhich means the product has to be sellable in the fifteen minutes\\nbetween lunch and dinner prep, by someone who is tired.\\n\\nThat's not a marketing constraint. It's a product constraint, and\\nit should change what you build.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 215,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-24\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B45\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 45,\n    \"title\": \"Recording\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Whether an employee can legally record a conversation with you\\ndepends on which state you're standing in.\\n\\nMost states allow recording with one party's consent — the person\\nrecording counts. A minority require all parties to consent.\\n\\nTwo things follow.\\n\\nYour no-recording policy has federal labor law problems in some\\napplications, so it isn't the clean fix it looks like.\\n\\nAnd in a one-party state, the disciplinary conversation you're\\nabout to have may already be evidence.\\n\\nManage as though it's recorded. In a lot of places, it is.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 776,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-25\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D10\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 10,\n    \"title\": \"Generation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Guessing before you're taught the answer makes the answer stick\\nharder, even when the guess is wrong.\\n\\nAttempting to produce something — a definition, a solution, an\\nexplanation — before you've been given it improves retention\\ncompared to just being told.\\n\\nThe wrong guess doesn't hurt. It appears to help, as long as the\\ncorrection comes.\\n\\nWhich means the instinct to read the chapter before attempting\\nthe problems has it backwards. Struggle first, briefly, then\\nread. The reading lands on a surface that's already been\\nprepared.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 154,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-25\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B29\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 29,\n    \"title\": \"Age\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Age discrimination protection starts at 40, applies at 20\\nemployees federally, and runs in only one direction.\\n\\nThe ADEA protects workers 40 and over. It does not protect\\nyounger workers from being passed over in favor of older ones.\\n\\nWhere restaurants get into trouble is language. \\\"We want\\nsomeone with energy.\\\" \\\"Looking for a younger vibe.\\\" \\\"He might\\nnot keep up.\\\"\\n\\nNone of that has to be in a termination letter to matter. It\\njust has to have been said in front of someone who remembers it.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 493,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-26\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A11\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 11,\n    \"title\": \"Accrual vs cash\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The month you felt rich is usually the month you lost money.\\n\\nCash accounting says you made money when money hit the account.\\nAccrual says you made money when you actually earned it.\\n\\nA big banquet deposit in March for an April event is April's\\nrevenue sitting in March's bank balance. Spend it in March and\\nyou've spent income you haven't earned yet.\\n\\nEvery operator who \\\"doesn't know where it went\\\" is running the\\nbusiness on the bank balance instead of the books.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 156,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-26\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A40\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 40,\n    \"title\": \"Situational leadership\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The same management style that saved you with one cook will lose\\nyou another, and neither outcome was about you.\\n\\nMatch the approach to where the person actually is. New and\\nwilling needs direction. Competent but shaky needs coaching.\\nCapable and unsure needs support. Expert and motivated needs to\\nbe left alone.\\n\\nThe most common failure is running one setting on everybody.\\n\\nUsually the setting that worked on the person you were.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 606,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-27\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D12\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 12,\n    \"title\": \"Transfer\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The hardest thing in learning is not understanding an idea. It's\\nrecognizing it somewhere it isn't labeled.\\n\\nIn a chapter on sunk cost, everyone gets sunk cost right. Two\\nweeks later, standing in front of an over-ordered walk-in,\\nalmost nobody does.\\n\\nThe chapter provides the cue. Life doesn't.\\n\\nWhich is the argument for learning ideas attached to as many\\ndifferent situations as possible. Not because the situations\\nmatter, but because each one adds another route back to the idea\\nwhen there's no heading on the page.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 190,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-27\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A69\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 69,\n    \"title\": \"JIT vs buffer\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Just-in-time only works when your suppliers are reliable, and it\\nis the first thing to break when they aren't.\\n\\nThin inventory frees cash, cuts waste, and needs less space.\\nIt also means one missed truck is an 86 on your top seller.\\n\\nBuffer stock costs cash and space and buys you the ability to\\nabsorb someone else's failure.\\n\\nThe right answer changes per item, not per restaurant. Buffer the\\nthings you're known for and the things with one supplier. Run\\nthin on everything with three sources and a short shelf life.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1093,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C6\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Contract-first\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I stopped writing features and spent a week writing the rules\\nthe features have to obey.\\n\\nSchemas, boundaries, what each part is allowed to assume about\\nevery other part. No new functionality shipped that week.\\n\\nThe reason: every bug I'd spent real time on traced back to two\\nparts of the system disagreeing about what a thing was, and\\nneither had been wrong on its own terms.\\n\\nWriting the contract first makes that class of bug impossible\\nrather than findable.\\n\\nSlowest week I've had. Probably the highest return.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 87,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-11-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B13\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 13,\n    \"title\": \"Uniform deductions\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"You can require the uniform. Whether you can charge for it\\ndepends on what it does to the wage.\\n\\nUnder federal law, the cost of a required uniform and its upkeep\\ngenerally can't cut an employee's pay below minimum wage in that\\nworkweek, and it can't cut into overtime pay.\\n\\nWhich matters most for exactly the people you'd charge — new\\nhires at or near the floor.\\n\\nSeveral states go further and prohibit the deduction outright.\\nThe line is not the same everywhere, and the deduction is\\nusually smaller than the claim.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 209,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-01\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C35\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 35,\n    \"title\": \"Finishing\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I finished a business degree last week and the honest summary is\\nthat it made me more useful in a way I can't fully separate from\\nthe last four years of everything else.\\n\\nWhat it definitely did: gave me the vocabulary for things I'd\\nbeen doing by instinct, and the frameworks to notice when the\\ninstinct was wrong.\\n\\nWhat it didn't do: make me a better operator on its own. The\\nknowledge is inert until it's attached to a real decision with\\nreal money.\\n\\nWhich is roughly the argument for building something while you\\nstudy. The coursework is the cheap half.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 612,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-01\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B42\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 42,\n    \"title\": \"Documentation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The write-up you didn't do is the one the other side gets to\\ndescribe.\\n\\nContemporaneous documentation — dated, specific, factual, shared\\nwith the employee — is the single highest-return administrative\\nhabit in a restaurant.\\n\\nNot because it wins cases on its own. Because its absence lets an\\neight-month performance history get retold as a two-week\\nretaliation story, and nothing in your files contradicts it.\\n\\nSpecific beats harsh. \\\"Third late arrival this month, 22 minutes,\\ndiscussed 3/14\\\" does more than a paragraph about attitude.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 720,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-02\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A26\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 26,\n    \"title\": \"Price elasticity\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"You raised prices 6% and covers fell 3%. You made money.\\n\\nElasticity is how much demand moves when price moves. If demand\\nmoves less than price did, the increase wins even though you're\\nserving fewer people.\\n\\nMost operators only see the covers going down and reverse it.\\n\\nDo the arithmetic before you panic. Fewer people paying more is\\nfrequently the better night, and it's an easier night to staff.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 387,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-02\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A55\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 55,\n    \"title\": \"Sample size\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Three bad reviews in a week is not a trend. It might not even be\\ninformation.\\n\\nSmall samples swing wildly for no reason at all. Flip a fair coin\\nfive times and you'll get four heads often enough that you'd\\nnever notice.\\n\\nThe bigger the sample, the closer it sits to the truth. Small\\nsamples produce the extremes in both directions — which is also\\nwhy one perfect week doesn't prove the fix worked.\\n\\nBefore you rip up the prep system over a rough Thursday, ask how\\nmany Thursdays you're actually looking at.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 860,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-03\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D14\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 14,\n    \"title\": \"Chunking\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"An expert doesn't think faster. They think in bigger pieces.\\n\\nA new cook sees eleven separate things on a ticket. Someone\\nexperienced sees two patterns. Same information, same processing\\nspeed, radically different load.\\n\\nThis is why expertise looks like calm rather than speed, and why\\nit can't be transferred by explanation. The chunks are built by\\nrepetition, not description.\\n\\nIt's also why the expert is a bad judge of how hard the task is.\\nThey can't see the eleven pieces anymore.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 227,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-03\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A12\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 12,\n    \"title\": \"Depreciation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your combi oven costs you money every single month, including\\nthe months you don't pay for it.\\n\\nYou wrote one check. But that oven has maybe eight years in it,\\nand each year burns through an eighth of what you paid.\\n\\nDepreciation isn't an accounting trick. It's the honest version\\nof what equipment costs.\\n\\nThe operator who thinks the oven is \\\"paid off\\\" is the one with\\nno money set aside when it dies mid-service in year nine.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 171,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C28\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 28,\n    \"title\": \"Security, company of one\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Security for a solo founder is not a smaller version of\\nenterprise security. It's a different problem.\\n\\nI don't have a team to enforce policy against. What I have is a\\nhandful of decisions that determine almost everything: where\\nsecrets live, what the session model is, what the database will\\nrefuse to do regardless of what the application asks.\\n\\nI picked one session authority and one migration authority and\\nmade both non-negotiable. Not because I'm disciplined — because I\\nknow I'll be tired at some point and want an exception.\\n\\nRules you can't grant yourself an exception to are the only ones\\nthat hold at 1am.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 485,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B26\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 26,\n    \"title\": \"Interactive process\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The most common ADA mistake in restaurants isn't refusing an\\naccommodation. It's never having the conversation.\\n\\nWhen an employee asks for a change tied to a medical condition,\\nthe employer is generally expected to engage in an interactive\\nprocess — an actual back-and-forth about what's needed and what's\\nworkable.\\n\\nSkipping it is itself a problem, even when the accommodation\\nwould have been denied on the merits.\\n\\nYou don't have to say yes. You do have to ask.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 439,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A41\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 41,\n    \"title\": \"Value chain\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Break your restaurant into the steps that actually add value and\\nyou'll find two or three that don't.\\n\\nReceiving, storage, prep, cook, plate, serve, clear, clean. Each\\none either makes the plate worth more to a guest or it doesn't.\\n\\nThen there's everything supporting it — scheduling, ordering,\\ntraining, repair — which adds no value directly and consumes\\nplenty.\\n\\nYou're not looking for waste. You're looking for the step where\\nyour version is genuinely better than the place down the street.\\n\\nIf you can't name it, that's the finding.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 621,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A70\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 70,\n    \"title\": \"Total cost of ownership\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The cheaper dish machine is frequently the more expensive one and\\nthe invoice will never tell you.\\n\\nTotal cost of ownership is purchase price plus install, plus\\nchemicals, plus water and energy, plus service contracts, plus\\ndowntime, plus what it costs to train people on it — across its\\nwhole life.\\n\\nA $3,000 difference at purchase disappears in eighteen months of\\nhigher chemical draw.\\n\\nBuyers compare the price because the price is the number that's\\neasy to find. That's the whole reason the cheap one keeps\\nwinning.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1109,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-08\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C21\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 21,\n    \"title\": \"Mobile-first\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I built this for a phone because I've watched where training\\nactually happens, and it is not at a desk.\\n\\nThere is no desk. There's a phone in an apron pocket, four\\nminutes between the prep list and the door, and a screen someone\\nis reading with one hand.\\n\\nEvery design decision falls out of that. Short units. No typing.\\nLegible in bad light. Works when it loads slowly.\\n\\nMost software for this industry was designed by people picturing\\nan office, and it shows in the first thirty seconds.\\n\\nWatch where the work happens before you decide what the work\\nlooks like.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 359,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-08\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B10\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 10,\n    \"title\": \"Rounding\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Rounding time is generally permitted. Rounding down is not.\\n\\nThe rule courts have applied is that a rounding practice has to\\nbe neutral — over time it should average out, gaining employees\\nas much as it costs them.\\n\\nA system that rounds to the nearest quarter hour in both\\ndirections is usually fine. A system that rounds clock-ins\\nforward and clock-outs backward is a wage claim with a paper\\ntrail attached.\\n\\nCheck which one your POS is actually doing. Most operators have\\nnever looked.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 157,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D16\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 16,\n    \"title\": \"Testing as training\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The quiz isn't there to measure the training. The quiz is part of\\nthe training.\\n\\nRetrieval is what makes memory durable, so the act of being\\ntested does more for retention than the same time spent\\nreviewing.\\n\\nThis changes how you'd build a training program. Not: teach,\\nteach, teach, assess. Instead: small retrieval attempts\\nthroughout, spaced out, low stakes, with correction.\\n\\nThe assessment at the end is for the record. The ones along the\\nway are for the learning, and they're the part most programs\\nskip.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 261,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-09\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B39\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 39,\n    \"title\": \"Deductions\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Charging a server for a walkout is one of the most common wage\\nviolations in the industry.\\n\\nUnder federal law, deductions for cash shortages, breakage,\\nwalkouts, and till discrepancies generally can't take an\\nemployee below minimum wage for the workweek, and can't cut into\\novertime.\\n\\nMany states prohibit them entirely regardless of the wage —\\ntreating them as the employer's cost of doing business.\\n\\nIt feels fair. It's frequently illegal, and it is exceptionally\\neasy to prove, because you wrote it on the paperwork yourself.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 668,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-10\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A27\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 27,\n    \"title\": \"Marginal thinking\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The right question is almost never \\\"is this profitable.\\\" It's\\n\\\"is the next one profitable.\\\"\\n\\nAverages tell you about the past. Margins tell you about the\\ndecision in front of you.\\n\\nShould you stay open the extra hour? Total profitability of the\\nrestaurant is irrelevant. What matters is whether the covers in\\nthat hour beat the labor, utilities, and wear in that hour.\\n\\nBusinesses that think in averages keep doing profitable things\\npast the point where they stopped paying.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 401,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-10\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A56\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 56,\n    \"title\": \"Correlation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Sales are up on the nights your best server works. That does not\\nmean she's causing it.\\n\\nShe might be scheduled on the busy nights. She might be scheduled\\non the nights the manager who runs a tight floor is on. She might\\nbe causing it.\\n\\nThree explanations, one pattern, and the data can't tell them\\napart.\\n\\nThe reason this matters isn't intellectual. It's that you're\\nabout to build a schedule, or a bonus, on whichever explanation\\nyou assumed.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 877,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-11\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D18\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 18,\n    \"title\": \"Notes\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Notes taken to capture everything are worse than notes taken to\\ncapture the argument.\\n\\nVerbatim transcription requires no processing, which is exactly\\nwhy it produces no learning. You can write down a sentence you\\ndid not understand.\\n\\nNotes in your own words force a decision about what the thing\\nmeans, and that decision is the learning event.\\n\\nThe test of a good note is whether it's useful in six months\\nwithout the source. Most notes fail that immediately, which is\\nwhy nobody rereads them.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 295,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-11\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A13\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 13,\n    \"title\": \"Working capital\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Profitable restaurants close all the time. Here's the mechanism.\\n\\nWorking capital is what you have on hand minus what's coming due\\nin the next stretch. Inventory and receivables on one side,\\nvendor bills and payroll on the other.\\n\\nProfit is an opinion at month end. Payroll is a fact on Friday.\\n\\nYou can be up on paper for the quarter and still not make Friday.\\nThat gap is where most closures actually happen.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 186,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-14\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C14\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 14,\n    \"title\": \"Build vs buy, my version\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I build the part that's the reason to choose this product. I buy\\neverything else, and I had that backwards for a while.\\n\\nAuth, payments, email delivery, file storage — all of these are\\nsolved, all of these are cheap, and none of them are why anyone\\nwould choose my product.\\n\\nI spent three weeks once on something I could have rented for\\n$29 a month. The three weeks weren't the real cost. The real cost\\nis that I now maintain it forever.\\n\\nAnything that isn't the reason you win is overhead you volunteered\\nfor.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 234,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-14\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B23\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 23,\n    \"title\": \"Coverage thresholds\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Half of the federal employment laws people worry about may not\\napply to a single-unit restaurant.\\n\\nTitle VII, the ADA, and GINA generally start at 15 employees.\\nThe ADEA at 20. FMLA at 50 within 75 miles.\\n\\nBut the FLSA reaches nearly every restaurant, the NLRA reaches\\nmost private employers regardless of size, and OSHA covers you\\nfrom your first employee.\\n\\nAnd states set their own thresholds — several apply\\ndiscrimination law at one employee.\\n\\nKnowing which laws actually reach you is the first hour of work,\\nand almost nobody does it.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 385,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-15\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C7\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 7,\n    \"title\": \"Technical debt\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Technical debt behaves exactly like financial debt, including\\nthe part people ignore.\\n\\nYou borrow time by taking a shortcut. That's real financing and\\nsometimes it's correct — shipping this month can be worth more\\nthan shipping clean.\\n\\nThe part that gets ignored is the interest. Every feature built\\non top of the shortcut costs a little more than it should, and\\nyou pay that on everything afterward, forever, until you settle\\nit.\\n\\nI know exactly which shortcut I took eleven months ago. I've been\\npaying on it since.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 105,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-15\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B7\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 7,\n    \"title\": \"General Duty Clause\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"There's no OSHA standard for that\\\" is not the defense people\\nthink it is.\\n\\nThe General Duty Clause requires a workplace free from\\nrecognized hazards likely to cause death or serious harm —\\nwhether or not a specific standard exists.\\n\\nA recognized hazard is one your industry knows about. Kitchens\\nare full of them, and \\\"no rule covers this\\\" doesn't help.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 114,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A42\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 42,\n    \"title\": \"Stuck in the middle\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The most dangerous position in any market is the middle, and it's\\nwhere most independents land by accident.\\n\\nTwo ways to win. Be the cheapest, which requires real cost\\nadvantage — scale, volume, systems. Or be different enough that\\nprice isn't the comparison.\\n\\nThe restaurant that's a bit nicer than fast casual and a bit\\ncheaper than the steakhouse competes with everyone and is chosen\\nby no one.\\n\\nNobody decides to be stuck in the middle. You arrive there by\\nsplitting the difference on every decision for four years.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 639,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-16\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A71\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 71,\n    \"title\": \"Single sourcing\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"One supplier gets you a better price and hands them your\\nschedule.\\n\\nSingle sourcing concentrates volume, which buys leverage, better\\nterms, and a relationship where somebody actually answers the\\nphone on a Saturday.\\n\\nIt also means their strike, their recall, and their bad quarter\\nare now yours.\\n\\nDual sourcing costs a few points and buys optionality.\\n\\nThe rule most operators land on eventually: single-source the\\ncommodities, dual-source anything the restaurant is known for.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1127,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-17\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D20\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 20,\n    \"title\": \"Survivorship\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every business book you've read is written about the companies\\nthat survived, which is why they mostly don't work.\\n\\nThe traits shared by successful firms are only meaningful if you\\nalso check whether the failed ones had them too. Almost no one\\nchecks, because the failures don't write books and don't get\\nstudied.\\n\\nBold vision, relentless focus, taking the big swing — the\\ncompanies that died had those in quantity.\\n\\nYou're reading a sample selected on the outcome. Any pattern you\\nfind in it is partly the selection, and there's no way to tell\\nhow much.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 329,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-17\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A28\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 28,\n    \"title\": \"Comparative advantage\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your best cook should probably not be doing the thing he's best\\nat.\\n\\nIf he's faster than everyone at both grill and prep, the question\\nisn't what he's best at. It's where the gap between him and the\\nnext person is biggest.\\n\\nPut him where his advantage is largest, not where his skill is\\nhighest. Give away the rest even if he'd do it better.\\n\\nThis is comparative advantage, it's two hundred years old, and it\\nis the single most useful idea for writing a schedule.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 417,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-18\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C36\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 36,\n    \"title\": \"If this doesn't work\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Worth writing down while it's still uncertain: what I'd conclude\\nif this product doesn't find a market.\\n\\nNot that the idea was wrong. The problem is real and I've lived\\nit.\\n\\nMore likely conclusions: that I underestimated how hard it is to\\nreach independent operators without a budget, or that the buying\\nprocess I described doesn't exist at the size I targeted, or that\\nwhat people needed was a service and I built software.\\n\\nNaming the likely failure now is the only way to recognize it\\nearly rather than four years in.\\n\\nMost people define success carefully and leave failure vague. It\\nshould be the other way around.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 630,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-18\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B36\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 36,\n    \"title\": \"Non-competes\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The federal non-compete ban is dead. Your state law is the whole\\nstory now.\\n\\nThe FTC's rule was blocked in court, and in early 2026 the\\ncommission formally removed it from the regulations and shifted\\nto case-by-case enforcement.\\n\\nThat is not the same as non-competes being fine. Several states\\nban them outright for most workers, several more cap them by\\nwage, and courts everywhere scrutinize scope and duration.\\n\\nFor an hourly cook, a non-compete is usually unenforceable and\\nalways a bad look.\\n\\nFor your GM with the vendor list, the answer is a state-law\\nquestion with a real answer. Get it.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 611,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-21\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A57\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 57,\n    \"title\": \"Regression to the mean\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"You chewed out the crew after the worst Saturday of the quarter\\nand next Saturday was better. The chewing out is not why.\\n\\nExtreme results are extreme partly because of luck, and luck\\ndoesn't repeat. After an unusually bad night the next one is\\nbetter on average no matter what you do.\\n\\nWhich quietly trains managers that criticism works and praise\\ndoesn't. Praise the best night, next one's worse. Criticize the\\nworst night, next one's better.\\n\\nBoth are the same statistical artifact, and one of them is\\nshaping how you talk to people.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 894,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-21\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A14\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 14,\n    \"title\": \"Inventory turnover\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Every case sitting in your dry storage is money you already spent\\nthat isn't earning anything.\\n\\nTurnover is how many times you sell through and replace your\\ninventory in a period. High turns mean your money keeps cycling.\\nLow turns mean it's sitting on a shelf going stale.\\n\\nThe temptation is to buy deep on a deal. Sometimes right.\\n\\nBut a case discount you'll take four months to sell through is\\nusually a worse deal than the price on the invoice suggests.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 200,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-22\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C29\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 29,\n    \"title\": \"Saying no\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The most useful thing I've said to an interested operator is\\n\\\"this doesn't do that.\\\"\\n\\nEvery instinct pushes the other way. Someone is finally engaged,\\nthey want one more thing, and yes is free in the moment.\\n\\nIt isn't. Yes commits build time you haven't scoped, creates an\\nexpectation you'll be measured against, and pulls the product\\ntoward one operation's specific setup.\\n\\nThe no that keeps the product coherent is worth more than the\\ndeal it might cost — at least while the product is still\\nfinding its shape.\\n\\nAsk me again in two years.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 503,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-22\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B20\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 20,\n    \"title\": \"Contractors\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Calling your dishwasher a 1099 contractor doesn't make him one,\\nand the agreement he signed doesn't either.\\n\\nClassification turns on the economic reality of the relationship\\n— control over the work, opportunity for profit or loss,\\ninvestment, permanence, skill, and how integral the work is to\\nyour business.\\n\\nA dishwasher working your hours, with your equipment, on your\\nschedule, doing work central to a restaurant, is an employee\\nunder any version of that analysis.\\n\\nMisclassification reaches back wages, overtime, and the payroll\\ntaxes — plus whatever the state assesses on top.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 333,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D22\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 22,\n    \"title\": \"Second-order\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"\\\"And then what happens?\\\" is the highest-return question in\\nbusiness and it gets asked far too rarely.\\n\\nFirst-order thinking stops at the immediate result. Cut prices,\\nget more customers.\\n\\nSecond-order asks what follows. Competitor matches, margin\\ngone, volume up, kitchen strained, quality drops, the regulars\\nwho came for quality leave, and you're now competing on price\\nwith a worse product.\\n\\nEveryone can do first-order. It's fast and it's usually right\\nabout the immediate effect.\\n\\nThe advantage is entirely in the second and third steps, and\\nthey only cost you the discipline to keep asking.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 366,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-23\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B4\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Retaliation\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Retaliation is the most frequently filed charge with the EEOC.\\nMore than any single type of discrimination.\\n\\nThe agency took in over 91,000 charges last fiscal year and\\nretaliation led the list, as it has for years.\\n\\nHere's what surprises managers: it's independently actionable.\\nThe underlying complaint can be completely meritless and the\\nretaliation claim can still succeed.\\n\\nMeaning you can win the argument and lose the case, because of\\nhow someone got scheduled the week after they complained.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 75,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-24\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A43\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 43,\n    \"title\": \"Switching costs\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your regulars aren't loyal. They're parked.\\n\\nSwitching cost is everything it takes a customer to go somewhere\\nelse — learning a new menu, a new drive, the risk that it's\\nworse, losing the server who knows their order.\\n\\nYou raise switching costs with familiarity, relationships, and\\nknowing the regular's kid's name. That's real economics, not\\nhospitality theater.\\n\\nEvery new place that opens nearby lowers those costs for\\nsomebody. Which is why you find out who was actually loyal in the\\nmonth after a competitor opens.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 656,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-24\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A72\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 72,\n    \"title\": \"Agency problem\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your GM's incentives and your incentives are not the same, and no\\namount of trust changes that.\\n\\nThe agency problem is what happens when someone makes decisions on\\nyour behalf while facing different consequences than you do.\\n\\nBonus on food cost and you'll get food cost — possibly by buying\\ndown quality, cutting portions, and pushing prep labor into\\nsomeone else's line item.\\n\\nThey're not betraying you. They're responding to the measure you\\nchose, which is what measures are for.\\n\\nWhatever you incentivize, you'll get. Including the version of it\\nyou didn't want.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 1145,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-25\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D24\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 24,\n    \"title\": \"Falsifiability\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Ask yourself what would change your mind. If nothing would, you\\ndon't have a belief, you have a position.\\n\\nThis is the cheapest intellectual habit available and almost\\nnobody runs it.\\n\\nI believe restaurant operators will pay for training software.\\nWhat would change my mind: forty real conversations where the\\nproblem is acknowledged and nobody will pay anything for it.\\n\\nNow I have a test, a number, and a way to be wrong on purpose\\nrather than by accident.\\n\\nUnfalsifiable confidence is how people spend four years finding\\nout.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 403,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-25\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A29\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 29,\n    \"title\": \"Diminishing returns\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The fourth cook on the line makes less difference than the third.\\nThe sixth might make things worse.\\n\\nAdd labor to a fixed amount of equipment and space, and each\\nadditional body produces less than the one before it. Past a\\npoint, they start costing each other steps.\\n\\nThis isn't a motivation problem. It's the shape of the room.\\n\\nMost \\\"we need more people\\\" conversations are actually \\\"we need\\nmore line\\\" conversations, and the two have very different price\\ntags.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 433,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-28\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C22\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 22,\n    \"title\": \"Offline\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Half the buildings I've worked in have a dead spot in the back,\\nand it's usually the walk-in.\\n\\nSo the product has to keep working when the connection doesn't —\\nprogress saved locally, synced when it comes back.\\n\\nThat's real engineering cost for something no buyer will ever ask\\nabout in a demo.\\n\\nThey'll just quietly stop using it after the third time it lost\\ntheir place, and they'll never tell me that's why.\\n\\nThe features people request and the features that decide\\nretention are frequently different lists.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 378,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-28\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B33\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 33,\n    \"title\": \"OSHA reporting\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The OSHA reporting clock is shorter than most managers think and\\nit starts at the incident, not at the paperwork.\\n\\nA work-related fatality: 8 hours.\\nAn in-patient hospitalization, amputation, or loss of an eye: 24\\nhours.\\n\\nThat's a phone call, not a form, and \\\"we were dealing with the\\nemergency\\\" doesn't pause it.\\n\\nSeparately, most employers keep the 300 log and post the annual\\nsummary. Small businesses in some categories are exempt from the\\nlog — but nobody is exempt from the reporting deadlines.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 560,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-29\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C15\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 15,\n    \"title\": \"The gate\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I have one command that decides whether my product is allowed to\\nbe shown to anyone.\\n\\nIt runs the type checks, the test suite, a build, and a\\nscripted walkthrough of the demo path. If any piece fails, the\\nanswer is no, regardless of how ready it feels.\\n\\nThe point isn't quality. The point is removing my judgment from a\\ndecision I have a strong incentive to get wrong.\\n\\nEvery founder has demoed something they knew was fragile because\\nthe meeting was already scheduled.\\n\\nA gate you can't argue with is worth more than a standard you\\nintend to hold.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 251,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-29\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B17\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 17,\n    \"title\": \"Waiting time\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The dead thirty minutes before the doors open is almost always\\npaid.\\n\\nWaiting that's controlled by the employer and short enough that\\nthe employee can't use it for their own purposes is hours worked.\\nBeing on premises, in uniform, waiting for a delivery or a rush\\nis exactly that.\\n\\nThe instinct is to clock people in when it gets busy.\\n\\nBut if you told them to be there, and they're there, the fact\\nthat there's nothing to do yet is your problem, not theirs.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 279,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A58\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 58,\n    \"title\": \"Averages\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your average ticket time is 14 minutes and your problem is\\nentirely in the tail.\\n\\nThe average tells you nothing about the 8% of tickets that took\\n40 minutes — which is where every complaint, comp, and bad review\\nlives.\\n\\nGuests don't experience your mean. They experience their ticket.\\n\\nStop reporting averages. Report the 90th percentile, and manage\\nthat. The average will follow, and the complaints stop first.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 911,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-30\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A15\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 15,\n    \"title\": \"Standard cost\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"A recipe card is a standard cost, and most kitchens treat it as\\na suggestion.\\n\\nThe whole point of writing down eight ounces is so that when\\nfood cost moves, you know whether it was price or portion.\\n\\nWithout the standard, you have one number going the wrong\\ndirection and no way to find out why.\\n\\nNobody costs a recipe because they enjoy costing recipes. They\\ndo it so next month's problem is diagnosable.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 215,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-31\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D26\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 26,\n    \"title\": \"Reading\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Reading a textbook front to back is the least efficient way to\\nuse one and it's the only way most people are taught.\\n\\nBetter sequence: read the chapter summary first. Then the\\nlearning objectives. Then skim the headings to build the shape.\\nThen read, looking for answers to questions you now have.\\n\\nYou're not trying to absorb the chapter. You're trying to fill in\\na structure you built before you started.\\n\\nSame pages, a third of the time, and considerably more of it\\nretained — because you read it looking for something.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 439,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2026-12-31\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A44\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 44,\n    \"title\": \"Scale vs scope\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Two different kinds of \\\"bigger\\\" and operators mix them up\\nconstantly.\\n\\nEconomies of scale: same thing, more volume, lower unit cost.\\nBuying protein for four locations instead of one.\\n\\nEconomies of scope: different things, shared resources. Using the\\nsame kitchen and crew to run lunch, dinner, and catering.\\n\\nScale needs volume. Scope needs flexibility. The systems that\\ndeliver one often make the other harder.\\n\\nAdding a catering arm to chase scope while trying to standardize\\nfor scale is how you get both cost structures and neither\\nadvantage.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 673,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-01\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C8\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 8,\n    \"title\": \"WBS on my own product\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"I broke my own product into 53 pieces and found the four I'd\\nbeen avoiding.\\n\\nA work breakdown structure decomposes a project until each piece\\nis small enough to estimate and assign. That's the stated\\npurpose.\\n\\nThe unstated one is that it makes avoidance visible. The nodes\\nwith no estimate, no owner, and no start date are the work you've\\nbeen routing around for months.\\n\\nMine were all in the same category, and the category was the one\\nI'm least confident in.\\n\\nThe chart didn't tell me anything I didn't know. It just stopped\\nme from not knowing it.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 123,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-01\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B1\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 1,\n    \"title\": \"Training time\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": \"the strongest post in this set\",\n    \"body\": \"If your cooks do training modules at home on their phones,\\nyou may be accruing wages you aren't paying.\\n\\nUnder the FLSA, training time is generally compensable\\nunless ALL FOUR of these are true:\\n\\n- outside regular working hours\\n- genuinely voluntary\\n- not directly related to the job\\n- no productive work performed\\n\\nStation training fails #3 by definition. That's the whole\\npoint of it.\\n\\nMultiply the untracked minutes by your headcount, then by\\nthe lookback period. That's the number.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 20,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A30\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 30,\n    \"title\": \"Substitutes and complements\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Your competition isn't the restaurant across the street. It's the\\ngrocery store, the meal kit, and the couch.\\n\\nSubstitutes are anything that satisfies the same need. When their\\nprice or convenience improves, your demand drops, and you never\\nsee the transaction that didn't happen.\\n\\nComplements work the other way. The movie theater that closed is\\nwhy your Tuesdays got quiet, and nobody in your building would\\nguess it.\\n\\nTrace the demand, not the block.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 449,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-04\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C1\",\n    \"pillar\": \"C\",\n    \"seq\": 1,\n    \"title\": \"VRIO on your own product\",\n    \"figure\": 23,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": \"attach figure 23\",\n    \"body\": \"I ran a strategy framework on my own company and it told me\\nmy code is worthless.\\n\\nVRIO asks four things about a resource: valuable, rare,\\ncostly to imitate, organized to capture.\\n\\nMy codebase: valuable, sort of rare, and trivially imitable.\\nA competent dev rebuilds it in a few months.\\n\\nWhat actually passes: fifteen years of back-of-house\\nexperience attached to someone who can also ship software.\\nThat can't be hired quickly and can't be copied by watching.\\n\\nThe moat was never the product. Worth knowing before you\\nspend another six months on features.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/40-pillar-c.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 5,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-05\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B30\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 30,\n    \"title\": \"Equal Pay Act\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"The Equal Pay Act has no employee-count threshold and almost no\\nrestaurant has ever run the analysis.\\n\\nIt requires equal pay for jobs requiring substantially equal\\nskill, effort, and responsibility under similar working\\nconditions.\\n\\nTitles don't control. Two cooks doing substantially the same work\\nat different rates need a defensible reason — seniority, merit,\\nproduction, or something other than sex.\\n\\n\\\"That's what he negotiated\\\" is not one of the listed defenses,\\nand it's the most common answer operators give.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 509,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-05\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"D1\",\n    \"pillar\": \"D\",\n    \"seq\": 1,\n    \"title\": \"Retrieval\",\n    \"figure\": 1,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": \"attach figure 1\",\n    \"body\": \"Rereading your notes is close to worthless and it feels great.\\n\\nThat's the trap. Rereading creates fluency — the material\\nlooks familiar, so you conclude you know it.\\n\\nFluency is not memory. Recognition is not recall.\\n\\nClosing the book and trying to retrieve it feels worse and\\nworks dramatically better. Every study technique that feels\\nuncomfortable is the one doing the work.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/50-pillar-d.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 5,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-06\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"B14\",\n    \"pillar\": \"B\",\n    \"seq\": 14,\n    \"title\": \"Breaks\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"There is no federal law requiring you to give anyone a lunch\\nbreak. That surprises people in both directions.\\n\\nWhat federal law says is conditional: short breaks, roughly 20\\nminutes or less, are generally compensable if you offer them.\\nBona fide meal periods, generally 30 minutes or more, aren't —\\nbut only if the employee is actually relieved of duty.\\n\\nA cook eating at the pass while watching the window is not\\nrelieved of duty. That's a paid meal period whether you coded it\\nthat way or not.\\n\\nAnd plenty of states do require breaks. Federal silence isn't\\npermission.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/30-pillar-b.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 226,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-06\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"22:15\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"post-close\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"A59\",\n    \"pillar\": \"A\",\n    \"seq\": 59,\n    \"title\": \"Critical path\",\n    \"figure\": null,\n    \"pinned\": false,\n    \"note\": null,\n    \"body\": \"Not every prep task is equally urgent, and the schedule you're\\nrunning probably treats them as if they are.\\n\\nThe critical path is the longest chain of tasks that must happen\\nin sequence. Anything on it delays open, one minute for one\\nminute. Anything off it has slack.\\n\\nBraising is on the path. Rolling silverware isn't.\\n\\nWhich means the right question at 9am isn't \\\"what's left.\\\" It's\\n\\\"what's on the path,\\\" and the answer changes what you hand to the\\nperson who just walked in.\",\n    \"sourceFile\": \"content/20-pillar-a.md\",\n    \"sourceLine\": 926,\n    \"scheduledDate\": \"2027-01-07\",\n    \"scheduledTime\": \"14:30\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\n    \"slot\": \"lull\",\n    \"status\": \"queued\",\n    \"deferredToEnd\": true\n  }\n]\n";

